# The S3's `boot-idle` heartbeat triple is ELICITED, exactly as the classic's
# is — and this file is the asking.
#
# `esp32_memory_stats` (`lp-fw/fw-esp32s3/src/main.rs`, added to this image by
# M6 P04b / PR #742) is what prints `[stack] heartbeat:` / `[MEM] free=` /
# `[JIT] used=`, and `lpa_server` calls it on a project load, a project
# unload, a stop-all, a client `runtime_status`, or either side of a shader
# compile — NEVER from the five-second server heartbeat, which takes
# `heartbeat_memory_stats` instead and prints nothing at all. That split is
# the classic's and it is deliberate: a periodic printer would flood the very
# USB-Serial-JTAG link the transport runs on.
#
# ⚠️ This is the one thing about this payload a reader is most likely to get
# wrong from the C6's shape, because the C6 is the exception: there
# `[stack] heartbeat: high-water` IS the five-second heartbeat's own line, so
# a boot with nobody talking prints the whole sample. On the S3 — as on the
# classic — a boot with nobody talking prints no `[MEM]`, no `[JIT]` and no
# `[stack]` line at all, and a `boot-idle` arm without this file records a
# transcript with nothing in it that the payload exists for.
#
# `stopAllProjects` is the smallest request that reaches the call site
# (`handlers::handle_stop_all_projects` calls `log_memory` whether or not a
# project is loaded), and it is the SAME request (the `+1 "M!…"` bytes below)
# as `walks/v3-stop-all.script` — `the_s3_stop_all_script_is_the_classics_
# stimulus` checks the two files' requests against each other rather than
# trusting the eye. One stimulus over three chips is what makes one payload
# name mean one question.
#
# ⚠️ **The TRIGGER line is not shared with the classic, and has moved twice
# since this file was authored (M6 P08).** It names the last line of this
# image's `[INIT]` chain before it reaches for the filesystem — pinned as
# part of `HELLO` in `lp-emu/esp/lp-emu-esp32s3/tests/boot_idle.rs` — and
# that line is this chip's own, not a shared constant: the lp-link USB
# cutover (`lp2025/2026-09-27-0215-lp-link-usb-cutover`) renamed it from
# `[INIT] I/O task spawned` to `[INIT] USB link task spawned`, and M2's
# `io-thread` feature (the link task's own thread, `lp2025/2026-10-02-1918-
# io-thread-other-boards`) renamed it again to the line below. Keep this
# trigger in step with `HELLO` — `the_s3_stop_all_script_is_the_classics_
# stimulus` fails the moment they drift, by design.
#
# ⚠️ **This script is not what the live heap-budget gate reads any more.**
# Since wire proto 30 the S3's console is lp-link, and
# `scripts/heap-budget-check.sh`'s `esp32s3` arm asks for the triple with a
# `stopAllProjects` request over the hosted link once the board's hello
# arrives, not by scripting raw `M!` bytes after this trigger (the lp-link
# image does not read unsolicited `M!` text on the port before a host has
# brought the link up). This file remains the `boot-idle` payload's
# `host_script` for the RomUp arm and the classic cross-check above; it is
# not dead data, just not the path `heap-budget-check-chips-s3` runs today.
#
# ⚠️ **Until M6 P06 this arm's run cannot complete, and the sentinel is not to
# be weakened to make it.** After the trigger line the shipped image
# calls `mount_filesystem(flash)`, which sets `SPI1.cmd`'s `usr` bit and spins
# until hardware clears it; `SPI1` is an accept block on that machine today,
# so the spin never ends and the server loop the wire needs never runs
# (`lp-emu-esp32s3/tests/boot_idle.rs`'s
# `the_boot_stops_where_p06_begins`). P06 lands the flash controller and the
# ROM-up path; this file is written for the image after it.
#
# The known, unwidened gap, the classic's: this send lands 1 ms of emulated
# time after the trigger, where a desk host's select loop polls at 50 ms.
# That is host latency, and it is why nothing in the `timing` class is
# compared for this payload.
after "[INIT] io thread: stack 4096 B, priority 1, core 0" +1 "M!{\"id\":1,\"msg\":\"stopAllProjects\"}\n"
